  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species is native to most European rivers north of the Pyrénées and Alps, eastward to Ural and Emba drainages, Aral and White Sea basins; Black Sea basin in Europe and northern Asia Minor. Naturally absent from Iberian Peninsula, Adriatic basin, Italy, Greece south of Pinios drainage, Great Britain north of 54°N, Ireland and Scandinavia north of 62°N. Introduced to southern Norway west of Telemark County, Ireland, Spain, Corsica, Sardinia, Lake Cerknica in Slovenia, the Crimean Peninsula, the United States, New Zealand and possibly Morocco. A map of its non-native range outside of Eurasia could not be produced within the scope of this assessment, however.",
    "population": "This species is abundant and widespread, and there are no reports of significant population decline.",
    "habitats": "This species inhabits mainly nutrient-rich, well vegetated lowland rivers, backwaters, oxbows, ponds and lakes. Spawns on roots or submerged plants. <br/><br/>Lives up to 17 years. Spawns for the first time at 3-4 years. Fractional spawner. Spawns in April-July, when temperature rises above 15°C. Males assemble at spawning grounds and drive ripe females, often with much splashing, into dense vegetation to spawn. Eggs are very sticky. Feeds predominantly on plankton, terrestrial insects and plant material. Able to adapt to unfavourable environmental conditions by slow growth and small size at maturity (stunted populations).",
    "threats": "No major threats have been identified.",
    "measures": "No species-specific conservation actions are in place.",
    "use_trade": "Locally taken as a food fish and targeted by recreational fishers in some parts of its range.",
    "rationale": "The Rudd is very widespread and no major threats have been identified. It is therefore assessed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
